+*   $n$: Bit width of the depth value.
+*   $k$: Number of parallel clusters (hardware-dependent).
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Duplicate Depths:** The algorithm must handle duplicate depth values. The conflict resolution logic ($FO_{NEW}$) is critical to ensure deterministic selection and avoid errors in the final result [S1].
+*   **Sign Bit:** The sign bit is always 0 due to near-plane culling. Ignoring it reduces computational complexity [S1].
+*   **Variable $N$:** The number of Gaussians per tile varies significantly. The algorithm must handle arbitrary $N$ without fixed-size buffers that assume a maximum scene complexity [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Parallel Comparison-Free Sorting
-A tile-based, comparison-free sorting algorithm for Gaussian points that uses an Element Vector Table (EVT) to identify the largest element (LE) in O(N) deterministic cycles, avoiding the high memory overhead of radix sort [S1]. It is chosen when hardware resources are constrained and deterministic latency is required, as opposed to global sorting methods [S1].
+A tile-based, comparison-free sorting algorithm that uses an Element Vector Table (EVT) to identify the largest element in $O(N)$ deterministic cycles, avoiding the high memory overhead of radix sort [S1]. It employs concurrent and sequential phases with bitwise operations to filter elements, making it suitable for hardware implementation where low gate count and predictable latency are required [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** O(N) deterministic cycles per tile, where N is the number of Gaussians in the tile [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low hardware complexity compared to radix sort, which requires histograms, prefix sums, and scatters [S1]. The EVT requires O(N) bits [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves full-HD rendering at 129 FPS, with 3× higher throughput and 5.98× smaller area compared to prior 3DGS accelerators [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fixed per-output latency, no global synchronization, low hardware complexity, suitable for low-power AR/VR hardware [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires near-plane culling to skip sign bit logic; performance depends on the number of Gaussians per tile, which can vary significantly [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ deterministic cycles for sorting $N$ elements [S1].
+*   **Hardware Complexity:** Low gate count compared to radix sort, which requires parallel scans and prefix sums [S1].
+*   **Latency:** Fixed per-output latency, avoiding the latency spikes associated with global synchronization in full-scene sorting [S1].
